When a page doesn’t have system focus, Ladybird still treats the area
its top-level traversable would have focused as the focused one, so
focusing that area again fires no focus events. All three engines do the
same: Blink’s Element::Focus() compares the element against the
document’s FocusedElement(), WebKit’s Element::focus() against
Document::focusedElement(), and Gecko’s nsFocusManager::CanSkipFocus()
against the window’s remembered focused element when the window isn’t
active — so a focus listener that focuses its own target again never
recurses, with or without system focus. When focus moves to another area
without system focus, Ladybird still fires its focus events, as WebKit
can; Blink and Gecko wait for the window to regain system focus first.
